<Simple path="/nil/how-we-verify/" pageType="methodology" eyebrow="Our method" crumbs={[{ name: 'NIL rules', href: '/nil/' }, { name: 'How we verify', href: '/nil/how-we-verify/' }]}
  lede="Every rule on this site is a record in a database, tied to the document it came from, the date we checked it and who checked it.">
  <h2 class="sec">Where rules come from</h2>
  <p>We use primary sources first: statutes, association bylaws, official forms, regulations and a school's own published policy. News stories and law firm summaries help us find a rule. They are never the source we cite when a primary document exists.</p>
  <h2 class="sec" style="margin-top:30px">What the labels mean</h2>
  <ul class="plain">
    <li><b>Sources checked.</b> We opened the source and recorded the passage that supports the rule. A person has not yet confirmed it.</li>
    <li><b>Needs verification.</b> Something about the rule could not be confirmed, such as the exact wording or whether a page is current. Treat it as a lead, not a fact.</li>
    <li><b>Verified.</b> A second checker compared the rule against the primary source. The page shows the date.</li>
    <li><b>School-confirmed.</b> An official at the school confirmed the page.</li>
    <li><b>Sources differ.</b> Two official sources say different things. We show both and say which one we follow.</li>
  </ul>
  <h2 class="sec" style="margin-top:30px">How rules cascade</h2>
  <p>A school page shows every rule that applies there: state law, the athletic association or NCAA, the district and the school itself. When layers differ, the strictest one sets the bottom line and every layer stays visible. When a statewide rule changes, every school page under it updates from the same record.</p>
  <h2 class="sec" style="margin-top:30px">What we do not do</h2>
  <p>We do not give legal advice, guess at rules we cannot source, or fill silence with assumptions. Where the rules do not address a question, the page says so and points you to the person at your school who can answer.</p>
</Simple>
